## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates Azure Functions expertise for a practical tenant onboarding workflow, integrating stateless HTTP processing, Redis caching, Cosmos DB persistence, and Service Bus messaging. It also tests understanding of idempotency patterns, comprehensive error handling, and data validation in a distributed system context.

## Key Concepts

- Azure Functions HTTP trigger configuration and routing
- Redis caching implementation with TTL management
- Cosmos DB upsert operations using PartitionKey strategy
- Azure Service Bus topic publishing for event-driven architecture
- Idempotency patterns for duplicate request handling
- Robust error handling and appropriate HTTP status codes
- Request validation and data transformation

## Code Example

```javascript
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  // Implementation outline
  const tenantId = req.headers['x-tenant-id'] || req.body?.tenantId;
  const { name, region } = req.body;

  // Validate required fields
  if (!tenantId || !name || !region) {
    return { status: 400, body: { error: 'Missing required fields' } };
  }

  // Check Redis cache
  const cacheKey = `tenants:${tenantId}`;
  let cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);

  if (!cached) {
    // Cache miss - write to Cosmos DB
    await cosmos.upsert({
      id: tenantId,
      PartitionKey: tenantId,
      tenantId,
      name,
      region
    });

    // Publish to Service Bus
    await serviceBus.publish('onboardingNotifications', {
      tenantId,
      name,
      region,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    });

    // Cache with 120s TTL
    await redis.setex(cacheKey, 120, JSON.stringify({ tenantId, name, region }));
  }

  return {
    status: 201,
    body: JSON.parse(cached) || { tenantId, name, region }
  };
};
```

